<p>Israel’s parliament approved a law on March 30 making the death penalty the default sentence for Palestinians in the occupied West Bank convicted by Israeli military courts of carrying out deadly attacks deemed acts of terrorism. The bill passed 62-48 with one abstention, and Prime Minister Benjamin Netanyahu voted in person alongside backers including National Security Minister Itamar Ben-Gvir, whose far-right allies had pushed strongly for the measure. The law says courts may reduce the sentence to life imprisonment in “special circumstances” and sets hanging as the execution method. The move immediately drew legal and diplomatic pushback. Rights groups said the law creates a discriminatory system aimed at Palestinians, and the Association for Civil Rights in Israel said it had petitioned the Supreme Court to strike it down, arguing it conflicts with Israel’s Basic Laws. Britain, France, Germany and Italy expressed deep concern before the vote, warning it could undermine Israel’s commitments to democratic principles, while the United States said it respected Israel’s sovereign right to set its own laws but expected any measure to be carried out with a fair trial and fair-trial protections. Israel has not carried out a judicial execution since the 1962 hanging of Adolf Eichmann, making the law a sharp break from long-standing practice even as court review now offers one clear institutional check still ahead.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/dQlhZaFbaYWAGc5ehDOH_A_NqDcmysY8xvd7zHCCcSk_H3phvLWscjCMpZ9ljauFz2r-w2NOKnjaWMHXwAdPzH6xnM5-4qeIIYUL677QqpdO' alt='Israeli Prime Minister Benjamin Netanyahu and National Security Minister Itamar Ben-Gvir walk together inside the Knesset in Jerusalem on March 27, 2025.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Broader reach: Beyond military courts in the West Bank, the law also lets Israeli criminal courts impose death or life imprisonment on anyone who intentionally kills with the aim of harming an Israeli citizen or resident and ending the existence of the state of Israel.</li><li>Deterrence debate: Israeli and Palestinian rights groups told outlets the measure is racist, draconian and unlikely to deter future attacks, challenging one of the core arguments advanced by its supporters.</li></ul><blockquote>The United States respects Israel’s sovereign right to determine its own laws and penalties for individuals convicted of terrorism. - State Department spokesperson</blockquote><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>Association for Civil Rights in Israel: The group argued the legislation conflicts with Israel’s Basic Laws, which prohibit arbitrary discrimination, and petitioned the Supreme Court to annul it. (<a href='https://www.dawn.com/news/1987110/us-says-respects-israel-right-to-determine-its-laws-after-death-penalty-for-palestinian-attackers'>Dawn</a>)</li><li>Legal experts: Experts quoted by The New York Times said the law was written in a way that makes it unlikely to apply to Jewish extremists who commit similar crimes. (<a href='https://www.nytimes.com/2026/03/30/world/middleeast/israel-death-penalty-palestinians-attacks.html'>The New York Times</a>)</li><li>Supporters in Israel’s governing right: Backers led by Itamar Ben-Gvir presented the law as a long-promised hard-line response to deadly attacks on Israelis. (<a href='https://www.pbs.org/newshour/world/israels-parliament-approves-death-penalty-for-palestinians-convicted-of-murdering-israelis'>PBS NewsHour</a>)</li></ul><h3>Sources:</h3><ul><li><a href='https://www.lemonde.fr/en/international/article/2026/03/31/with-god-s-help-we-will-kill-our-enemies-israel-introduces-the-death-penalty-for-palestinians-accused-of-terrorist-murder_6751965_4.html'>&#x27;With God&#x27;s help, we will kill our enemies&#x27;: Israel introduces the death penalty for Palestinians accused of &#x27;terrorist&#x27; murders</a> - lemonde.fr</li><li><a href='https://www.dawn.com/news/1987110/us-says-respects-israel-right-to-determine-its-laws-after-death-pen